TEXT 560 -120 Left 5 ;1) Open sw1 and sw2 (tstop=1m) \n2) Meas the current to the lines, divide by sqrt(2)/multiply by 3\n3) Set the IN parameter of the petersen to 120A\n4) Set the sw1 and sw2 again to tstop=1000\n \nSet now tstop=1s of sw4 \nand tstop=1.5s of sw2\n \nYou can now see that the current flows now from line1 to transformer1\nto the load and to the transformer2 and to the shunt reactor. \nThe current RMS of transformer1  is about 250A\n(and so appr. 15% over the nominal current of the transformer)\nThe current of transformer2 is smaller since there is no additional load\nThe current to the shunt reactor is much smaller after 1.5s because the\nvoltage level sinks from 115kV to 74kV\n \nYou can see that the voltage at the left bus2 is beginning with 1.5s oscillating\nThis is because of the changing petersen voltage and current situation\nPet1 has now a higher voltage and current and Pet2 has no current at all\nbecause the petersen is isolated from the circuit\nThe voltage at the pet1 goes to a final small value and because of the single\npetersen the circuit is now not ideal compensated\nLet us see how things changes if both petersen are connected to the same bus\nThis can be done by giving the same node name to both pet connection\n(see Rueckwaertsspeisung_petbus.asc)
TEXT 560 -176 Left 5 ;Start with parameter tstop=1000s of all switches\nLine1 has CE1=401n (small asymetric capacitance for petersen compensation)
